Name the timeval members with the types POSIX gives them, not glibc's.

media/gpu/v4l2 is compiled for the first time by this fork, and it is
the first Chromium code here to reach for __time_t and __suseconds_t.
Those are glibc's internal spellings -- reserved names it declares in
bits/types.h and then typedefs time_t and suseconds_t to. musl declares
only the public names, so on Alpine the cast targets do not exist and
the file does not compile:

  error: unknown type name '__time_t'; did you mean 'time_t'?
  error: unknown type name '__suseconds_t'; did you mean 'suseconds_t'?

The compiler's suggestion is the whole fix. TimeDeltaToTimeVal returns
struct timeval, so time_t and suseconds_t are by definition the types
of the two members being initialized, on either libc -- on glibc they
are the very typedefs __time_t and __suseconds_t resolve to, so this
is a no-op there rather than a musl-only shim. The checked_cast keeps
doing what it was written to do: catch an int64 that will not fit the
member on a 32-bit target.

Upstream never saw this because V4L2 is a ChromeOS configuration and
ChromeOS is glibc.

--- a/media/gpu/v4l2/v4l2_utils.cc
+++ b/media/gpu/v4l2/v4l2_utils.cc
@@ -571,8 +571,8 @@
     tv_sec -= 1;
   }
 
-  return {.tv_sec = base::checked_cast<__time_t>(tv_sec),
-          .tv_usec = base::checked_cast<__suseconds_t>(tv_usec)};
+  return {.tv_sec = base::checked_cast<time_t>(tv_sec),
+          .tv_usec = base::checked_cast<suseconds_t>(tv_usec)};
 }
 
 std::optional<SupportedVideoDecoderConfigs> GetSupportedV4L2DecoderConfigs() {
